From 4bced5b8c8e4ed5d7f1c89c5c48d5b3046a7ed16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: danilo neves cruz <cruzdanilo@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 18:53:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: evaluate web scripts in an isolated world

Pages that lock down their globals, such as extension pages under LavaMoat, reject Maestro's script ("property \"maestro\" of globalThis is inaccessible under scuttling mode"), so no element on them can be read. Evaluate in an isolated world instead: it shares the page's DOM but not its globals, and the same world name returns the same context for each document. Properties that page scripts define on custom elements don't exist in that world, so a resource id is read from the attribute whenever its property is missing. A navigation can commit between creating the world and evaluating in it. A numeric execution context id is only unique within a renderer, so after a cross-site commit the old id can name a context of another frame in the new renderer, and the evaluation would answer from there. Evaluate by the world's unique context id instead, read once per document from Runtime.executionContextCreated and kept for the frame until it stops resolving; a replaced document then fails the evaluation before the script has run, with "uniqueContextId not found" once its renderer drops it or "Cannot find context with specified id" while the back/forward cache keeps it, so wait for the execution context of the document that replaced it, then create the world there and evaluate in it. Every wait after the first replaced document shares one response timeout, so a page that keeps replacing its document fails the evaluation once it passes, like a target that never answers. A session that fails while Runtime may still be enabled on it is dropped, so its events never reach a later command.
